<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
A <code>${expr}</code> interpolation inside one of <code>mkDerivation</code>'s
shell-context attributes — <code>buildPhase</code>, <code>installPhase</code>,
<code>postInstall</code>, <code>configurePhase</code>, etc. — uses a value
that flows from an untrusted source: a function formal whose name is not
in the conventionally-trusted nixpkgs vocabulary, the result of
<code>builtins.fetch*</code> or <code>builtins.getEnv</code>, or the result
of an <code>import</code> call.
</p>
<p>
If an attacker can influence the source value (e.g. by editing a derivation
caller's argument), they can break out of the interpolation and inject
arbitrary shell commands. nixpkgs's <code>lib.escapeShellArg</code> is the
standard defence; wrap any externally-sourced value with it before placing
it inside a shell-context string.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Wrap the interpolated value with <code>lib.escapeShellArg</code> (or
<code>lib.escapeShellArgs</code> for arrays) before placing it inside a
shell-context string.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>Unsafe — a function formal flows directly into a build-phase string:</p>
<sample language="nix">
{ stdenv, lib, version }:
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  pname = "demo";
  inherit version;
  buildPhase = ''
    ./configure --version="${version}"
  '';
}
</sample>
<p>Safe — the value is escaped before interpolation:</p>
<sample language="nix">
{ stdenv, lib, version }:
stdenv.mkDerivation {
  pname = "demo";
  inherit version;
  buildPhase = ''
    ./configure --version=${lib.escapeShellArg version}
  '';
}
</sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>
nixpkgs Reference Manual:
<a href="https://nixos.org/manual/nixpkgs/stable/#sec-functions-library-strings">
lib.escapeShellArg</a>
</li>
<li>
CWE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/77.html">CWE-77:
Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in a Command</a>
</li>
<li>
CWE: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html">CWE-78:
Improper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command</a>
</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
